With a lull in the rain I go out and fotog other backpackers crapped out in nasty weather.  It's hard to believe that two people and two big dogs are in this tent.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
TRIP 21  JOURNAL&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
DAY FOUR&lt;br /&gt;
  An early morning report from the field saying all is well at 1:42 am. The twin creeks remind me of the Burnthouse/Upper Creek camp in Pisgah, a higher and a lower sound with the camp in the middle. Johnny B would understand and hopefully he'll get to experience soon enough. 14 plus nights in the holy land of the Slickrock. A mellow yet monster trip fit for 2 true dharma bum brothers on the Nylon Road. Now it is later but still early light and time to quit dreaming of bassoon players and all things sundry and think about a full day of spring. A mouse or squirrel got my food bag but only tore into the chocolate ziploc making it one buzzed out rodent. Why not hit the open dog pack with the dog food? Good eating there. I'll never understand the rodent. I wonder how the couple at the lake is doing, will they pack it up today and slowly wind their way upriver? It's of no real concern to me. I sit waiting for the sun to come and do the drying thing before moving out as it would be wise to gear up by 9 or 9:30 though maybe that's too early for the sun in this wet jungle gorge. I just hope it doesn't start raining this morning.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
                         HUNGOVER ATOP THE HANGOVER&lt;br /&gt;
    Day 4, one thousand years later. Man, what a worthy wet hike! I left Slickrock creek around 8:30 and humped up the endless Big Fat Gap trail, the longest mile in my life, until I saw the wonderful opening of the gravel lot in the gap. Here I quickly went down Windy Gap trail to fetch water and then returned to the gap and to the trailhead of the Hangover Lead South trail. An all day drizzle but I didn't seem to mind. This trail is rough but doable and fairly quick. The first section is a short switchback into a long steep pull to a cave overhang whereupon the trail levels and enters a wonderful grassy gap and further a level hemlock forest. Past this the trail becomes steeper and gnarly with rock steps and roots and pools of water and brambles. It finally reaches a heath opening where breaks are welcomed but it was here that the rain poured forth in a bitchy downpour and soaked everything and became a true test of my OR pack cover. Finally I reached Saddle Tree gap where I first thought to camp but said no way in the rain so I turned right and headed up and over the Haoe and down to Naked Ground and lo! Another tent! A girl and a guy were talking in a yellow REI dome tent as I scrambled to put up my shelter. I still don't know if they know I'm here or not.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
    THE RAIN CONTINUES TO fall so we won't be checking each other out anytime soon. The pack cover worked okay and my Marmot bag is good so all is dry! I pray this will let up soon and let me hang my shorts and t-shirt and tent stuff sack and pack cover. Until then I'm captive behind nylon bars. It's good to have company although it is early in the day and they may get bored and move as who wouldn't? I sure don't know how long I can last cooped up in this thing but harken to a clearing brighter sky as all this will pass. My last visit here was the same with rain and tentsite pools not fit for any tent in cool wet foggy conditions though I do not suffer alone this time. My snack foods are going quick and I'll have to cook to have some real meals though the rain causes disinterest. Slowly, very slowly, the rain tapers off and I puff my pipe and wait. I saw the &quot;boy&quot;, a 50ish geezor with bald pate and the woman I hope is his wife. They must be danged compatible since two dogs also popped out for a pee. How much room do they have in that tent?&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
    ME, I'M GOING STIR CRAZY and near meltdown here among the raindrops and weeds and trail ponds and wet nylon and fig newtons and gap debris and cold wet socks and Pat Boone shoes and impending blue skies filled with more rain and the squeak of her incessant voice and the pitiful sigh of a soaked Shunka near a wadded up t-shirt and hairties and a green nylon pair of oversized underwear that my Dad used to wear 25 years ago and a smelly old black ballcap with an unidentifiable whiteish greasy substance around the inside band. In other words, enjoying my stay in the beautiful Slickrock wilderness area.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
    THE STUFF STOPPED AND I went out to pee and took 2 photos, no sense in listing the pics as we'll all see them soon enough. There's still a lot of leaf drip so I will stay inside until I need to cook. What a miserable trip with rain every day, four days in a row and no promise of sun but it's all part of the wilderness experience. What really needs to be kept dry? Damn near everything. You should of heard me cursing going up to Saddle Tree, I think I scared the bears off. The couple in the tent will be leaving this splendid spot tomorrow as will I, probably. The wind coming up is a good sign and portents blue skies and health. The last two hours of socializing has got me worn down seven times worse than the Hangover Lead trail. I've got to keep my mouth shut and listen, just listen. Ancient history and so now I think about the Bob and points elsewhere.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
-Tipi Walter
